```java
// BROKEN: mutable config read/written directly, needs a lock on every access
class Config {
    private Map<String, String> settings = new HashMap<>();
    void set(String k, String v) { settings.put(k, v); }   // racy without a lock
    String get(String k) { return settings.get(k); }
}

// FIXED: immutable snapshot, swapped atomically
final class ImmutableConfig {
    private final Map<String, String> settings;
    ImmutableConfig(Map<String, String> settings) {
        this.settings = Map.copyOf(settings);
    }
    String get(String k) { return settings.get(k); }
}

class ConfigHolder {
    private final AtomicReference<ImmutableConfig> current;
    void reload(Map<String, String> newSettings) {
        current.set(new ImmutableConfig(newSettings));   // one atomic swap
    }
    String get(String k) { return current.get().get(k); }
}
```

Readers never lock — they grab whatever `ImmutableConfig` instance `current` points to and read freely, since that instance can never change underneath them. A reload builds a brand-new instance and swaps the reference atomically; readers see either the old config or the new one, never a half-updated one.

Immutability has a real cost: every logical change constructs an entirely new object. For a config with a dozen keys, `Map.copyOf` is essentially free. For a bigger structure, that stops being true:

```java
// A 10-million-entry immutable index: expensive to reconstruct per update
final class ImmutableIndex {
    private final Map<String, List<Long>> index;
    ImmutableIndex(Map<String, List<Long>> index) {
        this.index = Map.copyOf(index);   // O(n) copy of 10M entries, per update
    }
}
```

Copying 10 million entries on every single update is where the naive "just make it immutable" answer breaks down — the fix isn't abandoning immutability, it's using a data structure designed to share structure between versions (next slide).
